    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nAlthough an actual implementation is probably quite concise, I don't think having different preview widths for directory and files is a good idea for the following reasons:\r\n- It increases (already quite large) config size.\r\n- It introduces visual flicker when navigating down with `j`. Not sure how inconvenient this might be, but I don't think I like it in general.\r\n\r\nThe main idea for previewing file is mostly to have a general idea if the file is the one user expects it to be, not seeing all the details of the file in question. So (in my opinion) having a wide preview is not really a good idea, which can be seen in the default value of `config.windows.window_preview`.\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
